// @test: typecheck-fail
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: types, pure, purity, order, E503, T0985
// @expected-error: E503
// @description: A pure function calling an impure one declared LATER is refused, exactly as when it is declared first

// The callee is written BELOW its caller on purpose. With the two
// declarations swapped this file was already refused; in this order it
// was ACCEPTED — the same program, the same rule, and the verdict
// decided by which line came first (T0985).
//
// `pure` is a checked claim, and that is the whole argument for it over
// a naming convention. A claim checked or not depending on the author's
// editing order is not checked.
//
// MECHANISM: properties were registered as the walker went, in source
// order, and the `Call` arm treats a callee it cannot find as
// contributing NOTHING — so a forward reference read as pure. The
// module walk already registers function SIGNATURES in a phase of its
// own, precisely so forward references resolve; properties never got
// that phase, and now do.
//
// The sibling `purity_violation_io_is_refused.vr` covers the same rule
// with the callee above. Both must stay red-when-broken: a repair that
// only seeds one direction passes one file and not the other.

pure fn caller() -> Int {
    helper()
}

fn helper() -> Int {
    print("x");
    1
}

fn main() {
    print(f"{caller()}");
}
